<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Gilan</h1><p class="st-meta">1762 CE – 1786 CE (approx.) · capital Rasht</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived khanate on the Caspian coast whose ruler Hedayat-Allah Khan built up Rasht before falling to Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedayat-allah-khan">Hedayat-Allah Khan</a>, of a notable family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuman">Fuman</a>, became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire</a> around 1762 and soon ruled the province as an autonomous khanate with its centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> was rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and Caspian trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedayat-allah-khan">Hedayat-Allah Khan</a> developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a> as a commercial town. He was caught between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a>, the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan</a> and Russia, whose navy and merchants were active on the Caspian; he invited Russian help and briefly lost and regained his province.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1786 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedayat-allah-khan">Hedayat-Allah Khan</a> fled and was killed, and Gilan became part of the emerging <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 1762 CE – 1786 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.77%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1762 CE – 1786 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rasht</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hedayat-Allah Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gilaki, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gilan Khanate, Fumani khanate of Gilan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=khanate-of-gilan" aria-label="Khanate of Gilan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5921 836 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khanate of Gilan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6241 1092L6364 1269"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6364 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1092h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1092h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rasht. <a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=khanate-of-gilan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

